 The energy loss of a particle in a material is described by the Bethe equation, which is $$\Delta E = 
\frac{4 \pi N_{A} Z^{2} Z_{2} (Z_{1} Z_{2} \ln \frac{2m_{0} c^2}{T} + \frac{1}{2} \frac{Z_{1} Z_{2} (Z_{1} + Z_{2}) (Z_{1} Z_{2})}{A})}{(Z_{1} + Z_{2})^2} 
\frac{E}{m_{0} c^2}$$ where $N_{A}$ is Avogadro's number, $Z_{1}$ and $Z_{2}$ are atomic numbers of the nuclei, $m_{0}$ is the rest mass of the particle, $c$ is the speed of light, $T$ is the kinetic energy of the particle, and $A$ is the mass number of the nucleus. In the case of alpha particles, the particle is a helium nucleus with mass number $A = 4$ and atomic number $Z = 2$. When the alpha particle collides with a nucleus, the energy loss is therefore $$\Delta E_{nuc} = 
\frac{4 \pi (6.022 \times 10^{23}) (2)^{2} (2)(2)(2 \ln \frac{2 \times 1.66 \times 10^{-27} (3 \times 10^8)^2}{T} + \frac{1}{2} \frac{2(2)(2)(2+2)(2 \cdot 2)}{4})}{(2+2)^2} 
\frac{E}{1.66 \times 10^{-27} (3 \times 10^8)^2}$$ where $E$ is the kinetic energy of the alpha particle. In the case of alpha particle colliding with bound electrons, the energy loss is therefore $$\Delta E_{e} = 
\frac{4 \pi (6.022 \times 10^{23}) (2)^{2} (2)(2)(2 \ln \frac{2 \times 1.66 \times 10^{-27} (3 \times 10^8)^2}{T} + \frac{1}{2} \frac{2(2)(2)(2+2)(2 \cdot 2)}{1})}{(2+2)^2} 
\frac{E}{1.66 \times 10^{-27} (3 \times 10^8)^2}$$ 
\frac{E}{1.66 \times 10^{-27} (3 \times 10^8)^2}$$ The ratio of the energy loss of the alpha particle to the bound electrons is therefore $$\frac{\Delta E_{nuc}}{\Delta E_{e}} = \frac{4 \pi (6.022 \times 10^{23}) (2)^{2} (2)(2)(2 \ln \frac{2 \times 1.66 \times 10^{-27} (3 \times 10^8)^2}{T} + \frac{1}{2} \frac{2(2)(2)(2+2)(2 \cdot 2)}{4})}{(2+2)^2} 
